# ValidateTrajectory.srv

# 1. REQUEST (Input sent to the service)
# Desired EE trajectory path (flattened matrix or list of poses)
float64[] ee_positions_x
float64[] ee_positions_y
float64[] ee_positions_z
float64[] ee_quat
float64[] sim_time

# Configuration the arm is in when the trajectory starts, as
# [rail_m, shoulder_pan, shoulder_lift, elbow, wrist_1, wrist_2, wrist_3];
# the rail in metres, the six arm joints in radians. Same order as
# Validate_trajServer's joint_names.
#
# This is an INPUT, not something the server discovers. Inverse kinematics
# seeds each waypoint from the previous solution and the first from this
# pose, so a different start lands in a different solution branch and can
# change which waypoints are reachable. Sending it explicitly is what makes
# a validation result reproducible.
#
# REQUIRED. An omitted or wrong-length field is rejected rather than
# defaulted: a caller that forgets would otherwise get a confident answer
# computed from a configuration the robot is not in. Simulation and
# regression callers pass configurations.LEGACY_MATLAB_START_Q explicitly.
float64[] q_start

# Frame the ee_positions_* and ee_quat targets are expressed in. REQUIRED, and
# validated rather than assumed: the only accepted value is "rail_base_link".
#
# This is a field rather than a comment because getting it wrong is silent.
# The rail base is FIXED; the UR base_link rides the carriage and moves with
# the rail, so targets expressed relative to it disagree with the solver by
# the rail position plus the 25 mm carriage mount height. That is the
# discrepancy the solver docstring carried as a known issue, and a frame-free
# array of numbers gives nobody a way to notice it.
string target_frame

# The joint path to play, flattened [N x 7] in q_full order, one configuration
# per target, in the same order: the path the planner produced and offline
# validation checked, in its chosen winding. REQUIRED.
#
# The service used to re-solve the trajectory itself, greedily from q_start.
# A re-solve can follow a different solution branch from the one that was
# validated, so the robot could run a path nobody checked. The service now
# verifies THIS path against the targets, joint limits, velocity limits and
# collision, checks the measured start against its first configuration, and
# plays exactly it.
float64[] q_path

---
# 2. RESPONSE (Output sent back to the main script)
bool success
string message
float64[] joint_velocities  # Approved velocity commands (flattened)